### Mean-based versus rank-based comparisons

Mean-based and rank-based methods often tell a similar story when two populations have the same shape and differ mainly by a shift in location. But this is not always the case.

From before we saw that

- A comparison of means uses the **actual numerical values** of the observations.
- A rank-based comparison uses the **relative ordering** of the observations.

Let's look at two examples.

```{r}
set.seed(2817)

n <- 10000
```

#### Example 1: Same shape, shifted location

A mean-based comparison asks about the difference in **average numerical values**:

$$\Large
\mu_B-\mu_A$$

In this example,

$$\Large
Y_A \sim N(0,1)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
Y_B \sim N(1,1)$$

so

$$\Large
\mu_B-\mu_A = 1.$$

Suppose the two populations have the same shape and spread, but Group B is shifted one unit to the right.

```{r}
y_a <- rnorm(n, mean = 0, sd = 1)
y_b <- rnorm(n, mean = 1, sd = 1)

dd1 <- data.table(
  group = rep(c("A", "B"), each = n),
  y = c(y_a, y_b)
)
```

```{r}
dd1[, .(
  mean = mean(y),
  median = median(y)
), by = group]
```

```{r}
ggplot(dd1, aes(x = y, linetype = group)) +
  geom_density(linewidth = 0.8) +
  labs(
    x = "Outcome",
    y = "Density",
    linetype = "Group"
  )
```

Here, Group B has a higher mean and a higher median.

We can also ask a different question:

> If we randomly select one observation from each population, how often would the observation from Group B be larger?

Using the two simulated samples, we can estimate this probability by comparing every observation in Group B with every observation in Group A.

```{r}
mean(outer(y_b, y_a, ">"))
```

Because the distributions have the same shape and differ primarily by location, the mean-based and rank-based comparisons tell essentially the same story: Group B tends to have higher values.

#### Example 2: A higher mean does not necessarily mean "usually higher"

Now suppose Group A contains mostly low values, but also a small fraction of very large values.

Group B is concentrated around a moderately higher value.

A different way to compare the populations is to ask how likely an observation from one population is to be larger than an observation from the other:

$$\Large
P(Y_B > Y_A)$$

If the two populations are identical and continuous,

$$\Large
P(Y_B > Y_A)=0.5.$$

For Group A,

$$\Large
Y_A =
\begin{cases}
N(0,1) & \text{with probability }0.90 \\[4pt]
N(20,1) & \text{with probability }0.10
\end{cases}$$

while

$$\Large
Y_B\sim N(1.5,1).$$

The population mean for Group A is

$$\Large
\mu_A
=
0.90(0)+0.10(20)
=
2,$$

while

$$\Large
\mu_B=1.5.$$

Therefore,

$$\Large
\mu_A>\mu_B.$$

```{r}
y_a <- c(
  rnorm(round(0.90 * n), mean = 0, sd = 1),
  rnorm(round(0.10 * n), mean = 20, sd = 1)
)

y_b <- rnorm(n, mean = 1.5, sd = 1)

dd2 <- data.table(
  group = rep(c("A", "B"), times = c(length(y_a), length(y_b))),
  y = c(y_a, y_b)
)
```

```{r}
dd2[, .(
  mean = mean(y),
  median = median(y)
), by = group]
```

```{r}
ggplot(dd2, aes(x = y, linetype = group)) +
  geom_density(linewidth = 0.8) +
  labs(
    x = "Outcome",
    y = "Density",
    linetype = "Group"
  )
```

In this example, Group A has the **higher mean**, because a small number of very large observations pull the mean upward.

But most observations from Group A are still lower than observations from Group B.

Again, we can estimate the probability that an observation from Group B is larger than an observation from Group A:

```{r}
mean(outer(y_b, y_a, ">"))
```

Yet

$$\Large
P(Y_B>Y_A) > 0.5.$$

So these two statements can both be true:

$$\Large
\mu_A > \mu_B$$

and

$$\Large
P(Y_B>Y_A) > 0.5.$$

The two comparisons now tell different stories:

- Group A has the **higher mean**.
- Group B tends to have the **higher individual observations**.

> Mean-based methods use the numerical magnitude of the observations. Rank-based methods use their relative ordering.

When population distributions differ mainly by location, these approaches often lead to similar conclusions. When the shapes of the distributions differ substantially, they can answer meaningfully different questions.
